"Transactions of the Institute of British Geographers - Wiley Online Library (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1111/tran.70091)\nciteturn4search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: today; \n\n# The Inoperative Refusal: Gestures and How the Unruly Body Goes on Strike\n\nThuỳ-Trang T. Nguyen, \n\nDepartment of Geography, University of Wisconsin–Madison, Madison, Wisconsin, USA\n\nFirst published: 20 August 2026\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1111/tran.70091Digital Object Identifier (DOI)\n\nview metrics\n\nThe information, practices and views in this article are those of the author(s) and do not necessarily reflect the opinion of the Royal Geographical Society (with IBG).\n\n## ABSTRACT\n\nThis paper advances a way of thinking the body in human geography through gestures, their capacity to refuse inoperatively and their relation to unconscious processes. Extending insights from non-representational and embodied approaches to gestures in the field, it treats gestures as a distinct analytic and develops the concept of ‘inoperative refusal’ to account for how unconscious, corporeal practices interrupt axes of bodily discipline and surveillance without becoming intentional or representational forms of resistance. Through gestures, the body refuses. Drawing on a psychoanalytic reading of gestures via the work of Donald Winnicott, the paper reclaims the unconscious for the body and situates queer gestures as instances of the inoperative refusal. To examine how such refusal might operate collectively, I place it in dialogue with Herbert Marcuse's ‘Great Refusal’ (1955) and Maurice Blanchot's ‘pure means’ refusal (2010), which offer resources for thinking refusal towards non-programmatic forms of collective action. Moving beyond intentionality and negativity, I suggest that the inoperative refusal resides in ambiguity and indeterminacy, not the oppositional. The inoperative refusal affirms the open-endedness of the horizon of becoming while inviting a rethinking of solidarity as that which already takes place in the everyday attunement of bodies in gestures, where feelings of kinship and shared worlding may unexpectedly arise and exceed political identification.\n\n## 1 Introduction\n\nIn the hallucinatory verses of Notebook of a Return to the Native Land, Aime Césaire laments: ‘I have assassinated God with my laziness with my words with my gestures with my obscene songs’ (2001, 19). For Césaire, gestures of the oppressed commit the ultimate crime against the God of the colonial missionary. Because gestures express ‘ways of being in the world’ of the gesticulating person,^{1} the body and being of the colonised carry out such a crime without turning to a defiant state of mind or consciousness. Like the ‘laziness’ of the colonised and their ‘obscene’ songs, gestures can express a kind of underside refusal orchestrated by the body, which I term ‘the inoperative refusal’, after Jean-Luc Nancy's use of inoperativity (1991), to refer to the subversion of instrumentality, teleology or production, disentangling refusal from intentionality. For example, the ‘conversion symptoms’ of early psychoanalytic and neurological case studies—psychological difficulties made manifest in the body—appeared not as a declared position of the gesticulating person but as gestures: Dora's cough that interrupts speech, the involuntary movement that disrupts interaction or the stylised posture that exceeds intention (de Marneffe 1991; Freud 1905/2001). Instead of posturing towards a reading of psychopathology, I argue that such clinical cases exemplify a more widespread capacity of the body. I aim to show how thinking about gestures as such may lead us to an alternative politics of refusal. Recent scholarship in human geography has engaged ‘refusal’ as a concept for understanding how marginalised subjects and communities set limits on participation in, and incorporation by, dominant political and epistemic frameworks. This engagement draws on foundational interventions by Simpson (2007, 2014, 2017) and TallBear (2013), whose work on Indigenous sovereignty theorises refusal as a practice of withholding recognition and delimiting the terms of engagement with settler states, and by Eve Tuck and K. Wayne Yang, who frame refusal as a stance against extractive research logics (2014). Carole McGranahan further consolidates this approach by emphasising how refusal is generative rather than simply oppositional (2016). Human geographers have extended these insights into analyses of borders, migration and colonial power, as well as into methodological debates about fieldwork and the ethics of access, where refusal is understood as enacted through withholding, non-participation and limits on knowledge production (Guasco 2022; Jones 2012; Meier 2023; Wright 2018). Notably, for the emergence of negative geographies, Dekeyser and Jellis turn to refusal as a means of ‘staying with the tension’ and not simply reversing affirmationism to find a space for negative geographies (2021).\n\nAt the same time, there remains some ambiguity regarding the degree to which refusal may be understood as a conscious and strategic decision. Accounts grounded in Indigenous politics often emphasise refusal as an intentional act: a deliberate withholding, disengagement or non-compliance that asserts sovereignty and sets limits on recognition. Recent work in human geography suggests a different but complementary approach. Analyses of encounters within politically constrained environments indicate that what is interpreted as refusal can emerge through situated, relational and sometimes tacit practices. Jones (2012) follows the unauthorised border-crossing into India by a 62-year-old woman from Bangladesh, where she works as a servant for a wealthy family, to spend a month visiting her son and grandson. Jones's concept of ‘spaces of refusal’ shows how everyday practices of evasion and disregard can refuse to abide by the state's binary territorial and identity categories, even when they do not take the form of overt political resistance. Accordingly, refusal may involve partial disengagement, strategic ambiguity or forms of non-participation that are not always framed by participants themselves as deliberate acts. From this perspective, refusal may be better understood as a situated practice that is not reducible to conscious, intentional decision-making, including more ambiguous forms of disengagement under conditions of constraint. Following this thread of ambiguity, recent studies attending to gestures in human geography are concerned with how gestures articulate a certain politics of refusal by Black queer women in American gentrifying neighbourhoods (Adeyemi 2019; Seitz and Proudfoot 2021), by Palestinian people expressing humour and laughter in the heavily surveilled environment of border control security (Bhungalia 2020), and by people in Delhi taking over unfinished urban development projects ‘through practices of squatting and repair’ (Boudreau 2023; Simone et al. 2023).\n\nThis paper contributes to this thread of scholarship tracing the ambiguity of refusal through a theorisation of gestures as the medium through which the body goes on strike—unlike a conventional labour strike because it operates without intentionality—or, in other words, its inoperative refusal. To find one of the most sustained engagements with gestures and the connection between the psyche and the body, I will now turn to psychoanalysis and the writing of Winnicott. It is in this literature that I will argue for a reclaiming of the unconscious for the body via gestures.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nQuantitative Aspects of Security (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=cbe77ebcd43f50a96e87f7c41c3e7839674c021b&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn4search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; \nof P1 and P2.\n2.2\nSemantics of discrete-time CSP\nDeﬁnition 2.1 ([2]). (refusal) A refusal is considered either as a set X of events\nwhereby the process is in a stable state and each x ∈X is unavailable or as the\nnull refusal • which denotes the absence of refusal information.\nConsidering non-null refusal X, one should understand that the corresponding\nprocess is in a stable state and internal activities cannot be carried out.\nDeﬁnition 2.2 ([2]). (refusal trace) A refusal trace is an alternating sequence\nof refusals and events which starts and ends with a refusal.\nIt is important to state that the null refusal • denotes the absence of refusal\ninformation, while the empty refusal {} shows that the process is in a stable state.\nThe relevant set-theoric operators over refusals are given in [2].\nDeﬁnition 2.3 ([2]). (trace of a refusal trace) Let s = ⟨A0, a1, A1, ..., am, Am⟩\nbe a refusal trace. Then, the trace of s will be denoted as:\ntrace(s) = trace(⟨A0, a1, A1, . . . , am, Am⟩) b= ⟨a1, . . . , am⟩\nThe deﬁnition above states that trace(s) comprises no refusal information and\njust contains events of s. We will use the notation RJPK in order to represent\nthe discrete-time refusal traces model of CSP which denotes all possible refusal\ntraces of the CSP process P. The relevant axioms regarding RJPK can be found\nin [2].\nExample 2.4. The refusal trace\nMerih Seran Uysal, Seminar Quantitative Aspects of Security, WS 2006-2007--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGeneral Articles (https://journals.kabarak.ac.ke/index.php/klr/article/download/720/437)\nciteturn4search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; \nThis sub-section operationalises a three-stage deconstructive algo-\nrithm to interrogate the legal construction of marital consent in Kenya. \nThe first step is to notice how the law treats the wedding vow ‘I do’ as \nif it were a permanent grant of consent. This is what Derrida calls logo-\ncentrism: the reliance on a single, fixed origin point. The second step \nis to see how this idea is then written into the statute, turning consent \ninto something static and unchanging. Finally, when we read closely, \ncontradictions begin to appear. Each clause of the law both hides and \ndepends upon the possibility of refusal, revealing gaps that the text it-\nself cannot fully cover up. \nThese stages are then deployed across four analytical modules: the \nbinary opposition module (sovereign husband v submissive wife and its \nhaunting trace of refusal); the supplement module (logocentric origin, \n119\t Jacques Derrida, ‘Letter to a Japanese friend’, in Peggy Kamuf (ed) A Derrida reader: \nBetween the blinds, Columbia University Press, 1991, 270.\n120\t Robert Bernasconi, ‘The trace of Levinas in Derrida’, in David Wood and Robert Ber-\nnasconi (eds) Derrida and différance, Northwestern University Press, 1988, 15.\n121\t Bernasconi, ‘The trace of Levinas in Derrida’, 15.\n~ 148 ~--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThis is a repository copy of Removing redundant refusals: minimal complete test suites for (https://eprints.whiterose.ac.uk/id/eprint/173724/1/2021083898.pdf)\nciteturn4search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; \nRemoving Redundant Refusals: Minimal Complete\nTest Suites for Failure Trace Semantics\nMaciej Gazda\nDepartment of Computer Science\nUniversity of Shefﬁeld, UK\nRobert M. Hierons\nDepartment of Computer Science\nUniversity of Shefﬁeld, UK\nAbstract—We explore the problem of ﬁnding a minimal com-\nplete test suite for a refusal trace (or failure trace) semantics.\nSince complete test suites are typically inﬁnite, we consider the\nsetting with a bound ℓon the length of refusal traces of interest.\nA test suite T is thus complete if it is failed by all processes that\ncontain a disallowed refusal trace of length at most ℓ.\nThe proposed approach is based on generating a minimal com-\nplete set of forbidden refusal traces. Our solution utilises several\ninteresting insights into refusal trace semantics. In particular,\nwe identify a key class of refusals called fundamental refusals\nwhich essentially determine the refusal trace semantics, and the\nassociated fundamental equivalence relation. We then propose a\nsmall but not necessarily minimal test suite based on our theory,\nwhich is why quiescence is the only type of refusal.\nA refusal of a set X is observed through the tester offering\nthe actions in X and subsequent deadlock (detected through\na timeout). A failure trace [8], also called a refusal trace\n[19], and initially introduced by Philips [18], is a sequence\ncontaining actions and refusals of sets of actions. Clearly,\nan observation made in ioco is a type of refusal trace. It\nhas been noted that sometimes an SUT can block input. For\nexample, a web-page might have ﬁelds that are greyed-out or\nan autonomous system might switch off sensors. As a result,\nit can make sense to allow the observation of the refusal of\ninputs during testing, and this is reﬂected in a variant of ioco\ncalled mioco [12].\nThis paper considers testing from an LTS speciﬁcation\nwhere observations are refusal traces. The use of refusal traces\nwas motivated by several factors. First, in testing it is not\nnormally possible to backtrack or save the system state and so\nobservations are linear; they are decorated traces. Second, it is\noften feasible to observe refusal traces in testing. Indeed, there\nis a standard approach to observing the refusal of a set X of\nactions: one offers the actions in X and concludes that the\nrefusal has been observed if a timeout occurs1. Thus, from a\ntesting perspective it makes sense to consider refusal traces as\nobservations. Third, normally a tester will not have access to\nricher information than refusal traces since, for example, it is\nnot possible to identify the set of actions that can be executed\nin a particular state. Thus, refusal traces normally provide\nthe richest semantics that is consistent with testing under\nreasonably realistic assumptions [9]. Fourth, refusal traces are\nrequired if we wish to capture certain types of behaviour such\nas priorities [19]. Finally, the use of refusal traces is consistent\nwith the widely used ioco implementation relation.\nWe initially investigated the recent approach of Peleska\net al. [17], which concerned generating a ﬁnite test suite to\ncheck behaviour up to a given bound on length. This work\nTesting is arguably the most widely used form of software\nveriﬁcation and validation, but it is typically largely manual\nand therefore expensive and error-prone. There has thus been\nsigniﬁcant interest in test automation, including model-based\ntesting (MBT) approaches that base test generation on a formal\nspeciﬁcation or model [13]. There is evidence of the effec-\ntiveness of MBT in industrial projects [11] and, in addition,\nthe use of MBT approaches can guarantee that well-deﬁned\nclasses of faults will be found (see, for example, [7]).\nMBT work goes back to Moore’s seminal paper [16] and\ninitially considered ﬁnite state machine (FSM) speciﬁcations.\nHere, observations are traces: sequences of inputs and outputs.\nMore generally, however, observations may be richer than\nthis, leading to a wide range of implementation relations for\nlabelled transition systems (LTSs) [8]. The interest in LTSs is\nmotivated by their ability to capture the semantics of formal\nlanguages such as CSP (see, for example, [19]).\nIn testing, the consensus has been that observations are\ntypes of traces and so implementation relations are based on\ndecorated traces. Traces can include, for example, refusals,\nwhere a refusal X denotes the system being in a stable state\n1This is exactly what is normally done when testing based on ioco.\n978-1-6654-4895-6/21/$31.00 ©2021 IEEE--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRemoving redundant refusals: minimal complete test suites for failure trace semantics - White Rose Research Online (https://eprints.whiterose.ac.uk/id/eprint/173724/)\nciteturn4search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.3 years ago; Crawled: 2 days ago; \n# Removing redundant refusals: minimal complete test suites for failure trace semantics\n\nGazda, M. and Hierons, R.M. orcid.org/0000-0002-4771-1446 (2021) Removing redundant refusals: minimal complete test suites for failure trace semantics. In: 2021 36th Annual ACM/IEEE Symposium on Logic in Computer Science (LICS). Proceedings of the 2021 36th Annual ACM/IEEE Symposium on Logic in Computer Science (LICS), 29 Jun - 02 Jul 2021, Rome, Italy. IEEE. ISBN: 9781665448963.\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe explore the problem of finding a minimal complete test suite for a refusal trace (or failure trace) semantics. Since complete test suites are typically infinite, we consider the setting with a bound ℓ on the length of refusal traces of interest. A test suite T is thus complete if it is failed by all processes that contain a disallowed refusal trace of length at most ℓ.The proposed approach is based on generating a minimal complete set of forbidden refusal traces. Our solution utilises several interesting insights into refusal trace semantics. Personal use of this material is permitted. Permission from IEEE must be obtained for all other users, including reprinting/ republishing this material for advertising or promotional purposes, creating new collective works for resale or redistribution to servers or lists, or reuse of any copyrighted components of this work in other works. Reproduced in accordance with the publisher's self-archiving policy.\nKeywords:  | Computer science; Semantics; Refining; Test pattern generators; Testing\nDates:  | \n\n  * Accepted: 4 May 2021\n  * Published (online): 7 July 2021\n  * Published: 7 July 2021\nInstitution:  | The University of Sheffield\nAcademic Units:  | The University of Sheffield > Faculty of Engineering (Sheffield) > Department of Computer Science (Sheffield)\nFunding Infor