rawled: yesterday; \n\n  * ## Figures of Refusal\n\n#### Adam Haaga (bio)\nMemorial University of Newfoundland\n\nA review of Goh, Irving. The Reject: Community, Politics, and Religion after the Subject. New York: Fordham UP, 2014.\n\nMotivated in large part by Jean-Luc Nancy’s question, “who comes after the subject?,” Irving Goh’s book delivers a reply, provocatively arguing in favor of the reject, a figure resistant to the historically and politically contentious concept of the subject. Working among thinkers like Derrida, Deleuze, and Cixous, Goh systematically illuminates the suppressed figure of the reject from within current theories of the subject, attempting to drive “contemporary French thought beyond its existing horizons or limits” (23). To be clear, Goh’s proposed figure of the reject is not another concept among others vying to replace the subject, as if it were a substitutable concept. Goh instead prefers the phrase “figure of thought.” However Goh’s analysis balances and trembles between, on the one hand, the call for a decisive break with the subject as concept and, on the other, remaining dependent on it as material for rejection and auto-deconstuction from within the concept itself. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nModel independent refusal trace testing (https://eprints.whiterose.ac.uk/id/eprint/214702/8/1-s2.0-S0167642324000960-main.pdf)\nciteturn4search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; \nM. Gazda and R.M. Hierons\nScience of Computer Programming 239 (2025) 103173\nThe second condition is a natural property (we use the shortest refusal trace that reaches the initial state) and will be required by \nthe test generation algorithm.\nIf () is ﬁnite and the alphabet is ﬁnite then one can use a breadth-ﬁrst search to generate a state cover.\nProposition 4.1. Given language , if () is ﬁnite such that 𝑛 = |()|, then there is a state cover 𝑉such that:\n• 𝑉contains 𝑛refusal traces\n• 𝑉contains only fundamental refusal traces of \n• each refusal trace has length at most 𝑛\n• 𝑉is preﬁx-closed w.r.t. RTA\n• each refusal trace 𝑣 ∈𝑉has minimal length among all refusal traces in RTA ∩that reach  | 𝑣.\nWe shall use the term normal state cover for any state cover with the above properties.\nProof. That a state cover has |()| refusal traces follows immediately from its deﬁnition, requiring one refusal trace for each \nlanguage state. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nINTERNATIONAL REVIEW OF THE RED CROSS (https://international-review.icrc.org/sites/default/files/S0020860400090082a.pdf)\nciteturn4search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 16.6 years ago; Crawled: 16.6 years ago; \nINTERNATIONAL REVIEW OF THE RED CROSS\n\ndifficult when it depends on parameters such as the degree of danger or ethical doubts, areas in which the threshold of acceptability is particularly hard to establish. What will become of the French-led “ethic of refusal” remains to be seen, in view of MSF’s current efforts to strengthen its central structure and make it more international.\n\nDisplaying a lucidity which is at times disheartening, Rony Brauman occasionally indulges in sweeping generalizations, such as when he states that non-governmental organizations maintain an artificial climate of emergency to serve their own material ends and to keep a high profile. To avoid picking the wrong target, he might have done better to support his argument with a sharper reminder of the root causes of conflicts. In this sense, his systematic but rather reductive dismantling of the humanitarian phenomenon could ultimately have a demotivating effect. There is no denying, however, the relevance of most of his clinical observations, which show no trace of leniency. And if some admissions of failure seem to be attributable to passing moments of disillusion, they may also be a way of arousing our vigilance, which is so easily dissipated.\n\nJean-François Berger  \nICRC Delegate\n\nRecent publications\n\nAnnual Report 1995, by the International Committee of the Red Cross, Geneva, 1996, 346 pp.\n\nThe Annual Report gives a full account of the ICRC’s operational activities in 1995 and of its work to promote international humanitarian law, including the 26th International Conference of the Red Cross and the Red Crescent. The reader will also find useful information of a general nature on the ICRC.\n\n402--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n4 Comparing the three buffers (https://computerscience.unicam.it/diberardini/publications/reports/BUFFERS-TR.pdf)\nciteturn4search17 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 8 months ago; \n\nTo prove Item 1., we consider the refusal trace \\(v_1 = in(0)\\emptyset\\{out(0)\\}\\). Then \\(v_1 \\in\\) RT(Pipe\\((\\perp^{N+2})\\)) since (see Proposition 3.7 and 3.10)\n\nPipe\\((\\perp^{N+2}) \\xrightarrow{in(0)}_r\\) Pipe\\((\\perp^{N+1}0)\\) \\(\\xrightarrow{\\emptyset}_r\\) Pipe\\((\\perp^{N+1}0)\\) \\(\\xrightarrow{\\tau}_r\\) Pipe\\((\\perp^{N}0\\perp)\\) \\(\\xrightarrow{\\{out(0)\\}}_r\\).\n\nOn the other hand, Fifo\\((\\epsilon) \\xrightarrow{in(0)}_r\\) Fifo\\((0) \\xrightarrow{\\emptyset}_r\\) Fifo\\((0) \\xrightarrow{\\{out(0)\\}}_r\\) by Proposition 3.2.\n\nHence RT(Pipe\\((\\perp^{N+2})\\)) \\(\\not\\subseteq\\) RT(Fifo\\((\\epsilon)\\)).\n\nTo prove Item 2., we consider the refusal trace \\(v_2 = in(0)\\emptyset\\emptyset out(0)\\{in(0)\\}\\). Then \\(v_2 \\in\\) RT(Fifo\\((\\epsilon)\\)) since Fifo\\((\\epsilon) \\xrightarrow{in(0)}_r\\) Fifo\\((0) \\xrightarrow{\\emptyset}_r\\) Fifo\\((0) \\xrightarrow{\\emptyset}_r\\) Fifo\\((0) \\xrightarrow{out(0)}_r\\) Fifo\\((\\epsilon) \\xrightarrow{\\{in(0)\\}}_r\\) by Proposition 3.2.\n\nWe apply again Proposition 3.7 and 3.10 to see that this refusal trace cannot be performed by Pipe. The proof is more involved because the internal actions lead to a number of different cases.\n\nIn order to mimic the execution of \\(v_2\\), process Pipe must perform: Pipe\\((\\perp^{N+2}) \\xrightarrow{in(0)}_r\\) Pipe\\((\\perp^{N+1}0)\\).\n\nNow Pipe\\((\\perp^{N+1}0)\\) can perform some internal actions before the time step, i.e. we have Pipe\\((\\perp^{N+1}0) \\xrightarrow{w_1}_r\\) Pipe\\((\\perp^i 0 \\perp^j)\\), where \\(w_1 \\in \\{\\tau\\}^*\\) and \\(i+j=N+1\\), and then Pipe\\((\\perp^i 0 \\perp^j) \\xrightarrow{\\emptyset}_r\\) Pipe\\((\\perp^i 0 \\perp^j)\\). First, assume \\(i>0\\). By the first part of 3.10, Pipe\\((\\perp^i 0 \\perp^j)\\) has to perform at least one (urgent) \\(\\tau\\) before the next time step, i.e. Pipe\\((\\perp^i 0 \\perp^j) \\xrightarrow{w_2}_r\\) Pipe\\((\\perp^k 0 \\perp^{i-k} \\perp^j) \\xrightarrow{\\emptyset}_r\\) Pipe\\((\\perp^k 0 \\perp^{j+i-k})\\), where \\(w_2=\\tau^{i-k}\\) and \\(j+i-k>0\\).\n\n19--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) “I would prefer not to”: Resilience and Refusal in Literary Learning (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/399297587_I_would_prefer_not_to_Resilience_and_Refusal_in_Literary_Learning)\nciteturn4search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; Crawled: 6 months ago; \nChapter PDF Available\n\n# “I would prefer not to”: Resilience and Refusal in Literary Learning\n\n  * January 2026\n\nDOI:10.1007/978-3-662-71079-1_16\n\n  * License\n  * CC BY 4.0\n\n  * In book: Narratives of Resilience / Narrative der Resilienz (pp.329-346)\n\nAuthors:\n\nRoman Bartosch\n\nRoman Bartosch\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nImage\n\nDownload full-text PDFRead full-text\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe chapter uses a dual perspective on literary as well as pedagogical analyses of resilience discourses in education in order to facilitate more sustained exchanges between the environmental humanities and educational research. It argues that literary and other media texts that express memorable moments of refusal can aid the conceptualisation of a resilience pedagogy that refrains from neoliberal solutionism and instead fosters reflexion and contemplation. The keynote for this comes from one posterchild of political and philosophical literary criticism—Herman Melville’s “Bartleby the Scrivener”, whose iconic “I would prefer not to” has become subject of unending interpretive manoeuvres. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBook Review Essay: “Anorexia, Jouissance, and the Contemporary Clinic” A Review of Domenico Consenza’s “A Lacanian Reading of Anorexia” - European Journal of Psychoanalysis (https://www.journal-psychoanalysis.eu/articles/book-review-essay-anorexia/)\nciteturn4search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Crawled: last month; \nCosenza scaffolds his thesis through a reconstruction of the conceptual “flow” running through Lacan’s sparse, often elliptical remarks on anorexia across the span of his teaching. This effort allows for the articulation of the logic underlying the symptom’s core performative gesture, and for a mapping of the structural coordinates that this refusal responds to. The anorexic’s radical “no” is thus traced through four distinct positions Lacan assumed throughout the evolution of his thought. In the process, what emerges is not only a reading of anorexia but also an implicit masterclass in Lacan’s theoretical development, curated around the traces of a single signifier.\n\nCosenza takes off with Lacan’s earliest comments in “The Family Complexes” from 1938, where—still under Kleinian influence—the young Lacan conceives of anorexia as a refusal of weaning, a resistance to separating from the maternal rapport. This position, Cosenza notes, Lacan later abandons, only to return to it much later in his teaching, but reframed: not as a clinging to the maternal imago, but as a drive toward unity with the One—the omnipotent, seamless rapport that retains the trace of the maternal, is now articulated through Lacan’s critique of the discourse of the markets and its emblematic rejection of lack. Refusal, in Cosenza, amounts to a logic that operates on many levels: as metaphor, as defense, as an attempt for separation, and as pure jouissance of the body in its control over the drive, which is a symptomatic ideal of our times. Through the four functions of refusal Cosenza demonstrates how the positivity of the solution is enacted, laying the ground for a clinic that not only addresses the dialectical aspects of the symptom but fully engages with a new clinical horizon: a clinic of the Real, of jouissance, of the drive-body.\n\nRefusal, in Cosenza’s formulation, is far more than oppositional behavior or the byproduct of ego weakness. It is a complex, composite formation in which negativism and compliance are intricately grafted together—a singular strategy the anorexic subject deploys to navigate massive, unsolvable contradictions. She both defies and submits in her proclivity for complacency, revealing a psychic geography fractured between two poles: an absent, blind to singularity, evacuated Other and an excessively demanding, jouissance-driven, ever-engulfing Other. "
